WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.936
Ben Gunn’s boat—I soon discovered—was a very safe boat for a person my size.

2
00:00:05.937 --> 00:00:09.760
It was quite buoyant, but crooked and difficult to manage.

3
00:00:09.761 --> 00:00:15.129
Even Ben Gunn himself had admitted the boat was hard to handle until you got used to it.

4
00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:22.054
The boat turned every direction but the one I wanted to go, and most of the time I went sideways.

5
00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:31.553
But fortunately, as I paddled, the tide swept me along, and soon the Hispaniola loomed right in front of me, pitch black in the darkness.

6
00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:37.759
Slowly, I saw her spars—the poles supporting the rigging—and the hull take shape.

7
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.905
The closer I got to the ship, the faster the current grew.

8
00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:50.037
Soon I was alongside the ship and next to the thick, twined rope of the anchor line. I grabbed hold.

9
00:00:50.038 --> 00:00:55.748
"A few cuts with my pocketknife and the Hispaniola will float out with the tide," I said to myself.

10
00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:59.917
Then I noticed how tightly stretched the anchor line was.

11
00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:04.276
"The tension is so tight, it’s like a bowstring," I thought.

12
00:01:04.277 --> 00:01:13.846
"If I cut this line when the ship is pulling so hard against the anchor, my boat and I could be knocked clean out of the water. It’s too dangerous."

13
00:01:13.847 --> 00:01:18.220
But then a light wind started blowing from the southwest.

14
00:01:18.221 --> 00:01:22.753
The wind caught the Hispaniola , and the ship shifted position just a

15
00:01:22.754 --> 00:01:28.025
bit. I felt the anchor line go limp in my grasp and decided to go ahead and cut the

16
00:01:28.026 --> 00:01:33.862
rope. I took out my pocketknife, opened it with my teeth, and cut one thick strand after

17
00:01:33.863 --> 00:01:40.435
another. Soon the vessel was held in place by only two strands, which had tightened up once

18
00:01:40.436 --> 00:01:45.767
more. "When the rope becomes loose again," I said to myself, "I’ll cut these last

19
00:01:45.768 --> 00:01:51.077
strands." While I waited for a good gust of wind, someone opened a window in the

20
00:01:51.078 --> 00:01:56.859
stern. Something was thrown out and it splashed in the water—an empty bottle

21
00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:03.477
probably. "You stupid swab!" one man shouted. "I deserve a bigger share of the

22
00:02:03.478 --> 00:02:10.540
money!" I recognized the voice of Israel Hands and deduced that he was the other pirate left on the ship with Red

23
00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:16.503
Cap, the name I started calling the man in the red nightcap. They were drunk and

24
00:02:16.504 --> 00:02:22.113
arguing. "You’re a dog!" Red Cap shouted back. "You won’t get any

25
00:02:22.114 --> 00:02:24.764
more!" The men argued and cursed at each

26
00:02:24.765 --> 00:02:27.961
other. "They’ll be trying to kill each other soon," I

27
00:02:27.962 --> 00:02:31.257
thought. But they quieted down again and drank some

28
00:02:31.258 --> 00:02:36.295
more. Meanwhile on shore, the glow of the great campfire burned warmly through the

29
00:02:36.296 --> 00:02:41.318
trees. Someone was singing an old sailor’s

30
00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:54.841
song: "But one of her crew was still alive, Of the ship that sailed with

31
00:02:54.842 --> 00:02:59.082
seventy-five." At last another strong breeze came, and the ship

32
00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:05.612
shifted. I felt the rope go limp once more, and with a good, tough effort, I cut the last thick strands.

33
00:03:05.613 --> 00:03:11.112
The ship began to turn, spinning slowly, across the current.

34
00:03:11.113 --> 00:03:18.469
I paddled furiously to avoid being flooded by the ship’s wake, but I couldn’t get out of the way of the huge, turning ship.

35
00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:23.631
As the ship’s hull came close to Ben Gunn’s boat, I tried to shove myself away.

36
00:03:23.632 --> 00:03:33.214
But the effort was useless, and just as I gave one last push against the ship, my hands came across a thin cord that was hanging over the side.

37
00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:36.046
Instantly I grabbed it.

38
00:03:36.047 --> 00:03:39.349
"I’m saved!" I thought as the boat steadied.

39
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:43.825
I stood up in the boat and pulled my body up along the side of the ship.

40
00:03:43.826 --> 00:03:48.826
Immediately the ship and the boat began gliding swiftly together through the water.

41
00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:53.100
"Why don’t they notice the ship is moving?" I wondered.

42
00:03:53.101 --> 00:04:02.106
To satisfy my curiosity, I did something crazy—I pulled myself up just enough to peek inside the window of the captain’s cabin.

43
00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:11.364
One glance, and I knew the reason. Israel and Red Cap were locked in deadly combat, each with a hand upon the other’s throat.

44
00:04:11.365 --> 00:04:19.822
I dropped back onto the seat of Ben Gunn’s boat as the pirates at the campfire broke into another song, one that I’d heard so often:

45
00:04:19.823 --> 00:04:27.609
"Fifteen men on the dead man’s chest, Yo-ho-ho, and a bottle of rum!

46
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:35.947
Drink and the devil had done for the rest, Yo-ho-ho, and a bottle of rum!"

47
00:04:35.948 --> 00:04:43.283
All of a sudden, Ben Gunn’s boat quickly jerked left, zig-zagged, and seemed to change course.

48
00:04:43.284 --> 00:04:48.456
"What is happening?" I said to myself as the boat’s speed strangely increased.

49
00:04:48.457 --> 00:04:57.299
The Hispaniola seemed to stagger in her course too as she headed southward, her masts tossing back and forth against the blackness of the night.

50
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:06.209
I glanced over my shoulder. There, right behind me, was the glow of the campfire—but it was quickly getting smaller!

51
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.144
"I’m being pulled away from the island!" I thought in a panic.

52
00:05:10.145 --> 00:05:15.775
Bubbling higher, the water was sweeping the Hispaniola and Ben Gunn’s boat through the narrow, rocky strait

53
00:05:15.776 --> 00:05:20.434
between Skeleton Island and Treasure Island and out into the open sea.

54
00:05:20.435 --> 00:05:27.565
Suddenly the ship turned violently, and I could hear shouting and then feet pounding on the deck ladders.

55
00:05:27.566 --> 00:05:31.926
The two drunkards had finally stopped quarrelling and awakened to their danger.

56
00:05:31.927 --> 00:05:37.246
"Lord, save me!" I prayed as I lay down flat in the bottom of my little boat.

57
00:05:37.247 --> 00:05:41.734
All I could think of was being crushed on the jagged rocks.

58
00:05:41.735 --> 00:05:46.736
"I’m going to die!" I cried as I shut my eyes tight.

59
00:05:46.737 --> 00:05:57.735
I must have lain in Ben Gunn’s boat for hours, beaten to and fro by the waves, drenched with the flying ocean spray, and expecting death at every moment.

60
00:05:57.736 --> 00:06:04.250
Gradually, weariness and numbness overtook me, and I fell asleep in my sea-tossed little boat.

61
00:06:04.251 --> 00:06:12.257
I dreamed of home and the old Admiral Benbow.

